1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,894
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:01,406 --> 00:00:03,042
I'm Nidhin Jose.

3
00:00:03,372 --> 00:00:08,958
Today, I'm here to talk about platform
engineering for resilient retail

4
00:00:08,973 --> 00:00:10,778
supply chains with event-driven design.

5
00:00:11,816 --> 00:00:14,414
Retail systems are highly distributed.

6
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:21,183
A single order can cross order management,
inventory, fulfillment, logistics,

7
00:00:21,372 --> 00:00:23,486
stores, and deliver-delivery systems.

8
00:00:24,788 --> 00:00:29,289
For the customer, they see one
promise, but behind it there are many

9
00:00:29,289 --> 00:00:33,389
independent systems with different
ownership boundaries and failure modes.

10
00:00:34,119 --> 00:00:39,439
This talk is about the architectural
practices that make event-driven systems

11
00:00:39,461 --> 00:00:45,241
resilient at scale, like ownership,
reliable delivery, idempotency,

12
00:00:45,424 --> 00:00:49,827
consistency, observability, recovery,
contract evolution, et cetera.

13
00:00:50,678 --> 00:00:56,395
Platform engineering makes those
ca-capabilities reusable across teams.

14
00:00:57,258 --> 00:00:57,963
Let's dive in

15
00:00:58,963 --> 00:01:05,144
Resilience is a platform capability,
not-- it-- resilience come-- does

16
00:01:05,145 --> 00:01:09,655
not come from just introducing
an asynchronous messaging system.

17
00:01:10,233 --> 00:01:14,489
Messaging infrastructure gives us
the durability, decoupling, replay,

18
00:01:14,531 --> 00:01:18,876
et cetera, but it does not guarantee
correct behavior during failures.

19
00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:24,947
Real resilience come from clear ownership,
stable contracts, idempotent processing,

20
00:01:25,255 --> 00:01:29,464
controlled retries, observability,
replay, and predictable recovery.

21
00:01:30,299 --> 00:01:36,995
Without those controls, we, we have
not removed the failure, we have simply

22
00:01:36,998 --> 00:01:39,432
moved it into an asynchronous workflows.

23
00:01:40,141 --> 00:01:43,956
Platform engineering standardizes
these reliability patterns to

24
00:01:44,374 --> 00:01:49,076
teams do not-- so, so teams do not
have to solve them independently

25
00:01:50,076 --> 00:01:55,554
Each domain publishes
only the fact it owns.

26
00:01:56,354 --> 00:02:00,520
A resilient architecture starts
with a clear domain ownership.

27
00:02:01,183 --> 00:02:07,079
Order management owns the orders
lifecycle, inventory owns availability

28
00:02:07,079 --> 00:02:13,048
and reservations, logistics owns
shipments processing, ship-shipment,

29
00:02:13,056 --> 00:02:17,912
shipment progression, catalog and pricing
own product and pricing information.

30
00:02:18,406 --> 00:02:23,317
Each domain should publish only the
fact it is authori-authoritative for.

31
00:02:23,851 --> 00:02:28,558
So order management publishes orders
placed, inventory then publishes

32
00:02:28,558 --> 00:02:33,689
either stock
reserved or reservation rejected.

33
00:02:34,746 --> 00:02:40,915
This keeps event contracts meaningful and
avoid cross-domain ownership and confusion

34
00:02:41,915 --> 00:02:44,098
Commands and events are different.

35
00:02:44,412 --> 00:02:48,842
Commands can be rejected,
events record actual-- the

36
00:02:48,885 --> 00:02:51,535
actual scenario what happened.

37
00:02:52,053 --> 00:02:54,100
That is where events store that data.

38
00:02:55,177 --> 00:02:59,261
Commands express intent,
events record the outcomes.

39
00:02:59,814 --> 00:03:05,219
Reserve stock asks inventory
to perform an action.

40
00:03:05,454 --> 00:03:09,433
The inventory validates the
request and publishes either stock

41
00:03:09,461 --> 00:03:12,241
reserved or reservation rejected.

42
00:03:12,709 --> 00:03:16,303
Consumers can then react to
those completed business fact.

43
00:03:16,737 --> 00:03:20,057
Keeping commands and events separate
prevents hidden coupling and

44
00:03:20,073 --> 00:03:25,734
avoids re-re-recreating synchronous
request-response flows over message

45
00:03:26,734 --> 00:03:29,794
Let's talk about Kafka
in, in these use cases.

46
00:03:30,351 --> 00:03:34,123
Kafka allows producers and
consumers operate independently.

47
00:03:34,335 --> 00:03:37,828
Producers publish without waiting
for every downstream consumer.

48
00:03:38,272 --> 00:03:43,586
Each consumer groups-- each consumer
group process events at its own pace.

49
00:03:44,532 --> 00:03:47,591
Partition is, is crucial and important.

50
00:03:47,786 --> 00:03:52,422
If order level sequencing matters, order
ID can be used as the partition key.

51
00:03:53,008 --> 00:03:58,172
That gives us ordering within an
order, but not a global ordering.

52
00:03:58,208 --> 00:04:00,909
That's a very important point to remember.

53
00:04:01,696 --> 00:04:02,115
And

54
00:04:03,115 --> 00:04:08,063
And because the events can be
reprocessed after failure or rebalances,

55
00:04:08,083 --> 00:04:11,084
consumers must handle duplicate safety.

56
00:04:11,661 --> 00:04:14,230
Kafka provides transport and retention.

57
00:04:14,715 --> 00:04:17,663
business correction still
belongs to the application.

58
00:04:18,663 --> 00:04:21,703
The transactional outbox
closes the publish gap.

59
00:04:21,965 --> 00:04:27,319
The, the transactional outbox
address the dual write problem.

60
00:04:27,906 --> 00:04:33,386
If a service commits database,
database state but fails to pub--

61
00:04:33,626 --> 00:04:39,008
fails before publishing an event, the
downstream systems become inconsistent

62
00:04:40,008 --> 00:04:43,050
Publishing first create
opposite risk as well.

63
00:04:43,536 --> 00:04:48,042
The outbox solves this by storing
the business update and even

64
00:04:48,043 --> 00:04:50,345
recording the same local transaction.

65
00:04:50,861 --> 00:04:53,888
A separate publisher
then delivers the event.

66
00:04:54,474 --> 00:05:00,134
This improves reliability, but
duplicate publication can still happen.

67
00:05:00,587 --> 00:05:06,366
So reliable publishing and consumer idempotency must be designed together

68
00:05:07,366 --> 00:05:08,987
Assume at least once delivery.

69
00:05:09,488 --> 00:05:13,210
A practical assumption is that an
event may be delivered more than once.

70
00:05:13,534 --> 00:05:17,747
If stock receive-- reserved is a
pro-- is processed twice, it should

71
00:05:17,748 --> 00:05:19,331
not trigger duplicate fulfillment.

72
00:05:19,802 --> 00:05:24,189
Stable event IDs and business-level
idempotency protect against that.

73
00:05:24,647 --> 00:05:27,843
Failure handling also--
su-should-- failure handling

74
00:05:27,848 --> 00:05:29,133
should also be classified.

75
00:05:29,854 --> 00:05:33,130
Transient failures get
bounded retries and back-off.

76
00:05:33,198 --> 00:05:37,474
Invalid contracts or impossible states
should move to a dead-letter queue.

77
00:05:37,944 --> 00:05:43,713
And the dead-letter queue needs, d-dead
letter path needs clear ownership,

78
00:05:43,782 --> 00:05:45,969
alert inspection, and controlled replay

79
00:05:46,969 --> 00:05:47,713
Event sourcing.

80
00:05:49,586 --> 00:05:52,964
Event sourcing is useful when
history and reconstruction matter.

81
00:05:53,467 --> 00:05:57,785
Instead of storing only the current
state, we store the sequence of events

82
00:05:57,816 --> 00:06:00,199
that produced it as an immutable log.

83
00:06:00,647 --> 00:06:04,121
For inventory, that could
include receipts, reservations,

84
00:06:04,155 --> 00:06:05,333
releases, and adjustment.

85
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:09,367
This gives strong auditability and
allow projections to be rebuilt,

86
00:06:09,663 --> 00:06:13,974
but it adds complexity around
snapshots, schema evolution,

87
00:06:13,994 --> 00:06:16,395
replay, retention, and correction.

88
00:06:16,782 --> 00:06:20,041
So event sourcing should
be used selectively where

89
00:06:20,074 --> 00:06:24,886
the value of reconstruction
justifies the operational cost

90
00:06:25,886 --> 00:06:27,435
Let's talk about CQRS.

91
00:06:29,048 --> 00:06:33,163
CQRS separates authoritative
writes from optimized read.

92
00:06:33,483 --> 00:06:36,426
The command side protects the
business rules and invariants.

93
00:06:36,832 --> 00:06:41,908
Events then update and read mo-read
model designed for different consumers.

94
00:06:42,381 --> 00:06:45,580
A storefront may need a
faster avail-availability.

95
00:06:45,616 --> 00:06:48,329
Operators may, may need
a fulfillment dashboard.

96
00:06:48,759 --> 00:06:51,562
Analytics may, may need
a different projection.

97
00:06:51,875 --> 00:06:54,279
The trade-off is eventual consistency.

98
00:06:54,776 --> 00:06:59,383
Read models can lag, so critical
decisions still need validation

99
00:06:59,385 --> 00:07:01,383
against the authoritative state

100
00:07:02,383 --> 00:07:04,139
Let's talk about the Saga Pattern.

101
00:07:06,136 --> 00:07:11,418
Distributed workflows often span
mul-- span across multiple systems.

102
00:07:11,949 --> 00:07:16,443
For example, reserve inventory,
authorize payment, dispatch shipment,

103
00:07:17,114 --> 00:07:22,136
ex-- and et cetera, rece-- maybe receive
event, re-receive orders, et cetera.

104
00:07:22,542 --> 00:07:26,275
There is no single asset
transactions across all of them.

105
00:07:26,531 --> 00:07:28,582
A saga coordinates those steps.

106
00:07:28,847 --> 00:07:32,391
If payment fails after
inventory is reserved, we may

107
00:07:32,391 --> 00:07:33,862
release the res-reservation.

108
00:07:34,281 --> 00:07:39,148
If shipment has already progressed,
recovery may require cancellation,

109
00:07:39,224 --> 00:07:40,904
rerouting, or manual intervention.

110
00:07:41,525 --> 00:07:44,309
Compensation is not just
a database rollback.

111
00:07:44,472 --> 00:07:51,281
It's a new business action that reverses
or mitigates an earlier one, which will

112
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:53,489
be stored as an another event as well

113
00:07:54,489 --> 00:07:55,920
Ordering and consistency.

114
00:07:57,646 --> 00:08:01,780
Here in this case, ordering
is scoped, not a global order.

115
00:08:02,142 --> 00:08:06,292
Kafka preserves ordering within a
partition, but events from different

116
00:08:06,294 --> 00:08:08,436
domains may arrive in different sequences.

117
00:08:08,781 --> 00:08:12,497
A projection may see logistic event
before the related inventory update.

118
00:08:12,530 --> 00:08:14,398
The system has to tolerate that.

119
00:08:14,911 --> 00:08:19,007
We can use versions, transition rules,
buffering, and/or reconciliation.

120
00:08:19,145 --> 00:08:22,011
The key is to make consistency explicit.

121
00:08:22,518 --> 00:08:27,027
Teams should know which views are
authoritative, which may lag, and

122
00:08:27,035 --> 00:08:29,510
how discrepancies are, are repaired

123
00:08:30,510 --> 00:08:32,867
Let's talk about event contract evolution.

124
00:08:33,236 --> 00:08:37,052
Event contract should
be treated like APIs.

125
00:08:37,476 --> 00:08:42,596
adding an optional field is usually
safe, but removing a field or

126
00:08:42,598 --> 00:08:47,392
changing its meaning can be a, can
be a prob-- can break consumers.

127
00:08:47,702 --> 00:08:51,051
A matured platform should provide
versioning rules, compatibility

128
00:08:51,051 --> 00:08:54,786
checks, automated contract
testing, and migration support.

129
00:08:55,344 --> 00:08:57,672
The goal is not heavy governance.

130
00:08:58,107 --> 00:09:02,092
The goal is to detect breaking
changes before they reach production

131
00:09:03,092 --> 00:09:03,948
Paved path.

132
00:09:04,908 --> 00:09:07,824
This is where platform
engineering adds the most value.

133
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:13,381
Teams should not rebuild the same
reliab-reliability and infras--

134
00:09:13,416 --> 00:09:17,101
in reliability, re-re-reliability
infrastructure repeatedly.

135
00:09:17,578 --> 00:09:22,304
The platform can provide topic
provisioning, schema res-registration,

136
00:09:22,421 --> 00:09:26,695
access control, producer and
consumer templates, retries, dead

137
00:09:26,696 --> 00:09:30,301
letter queue handling, tracing,
replay, and contract testing.

138
00:09:30,806 --> 00:09:34,824
It can also provide governance around
ownership, retention, and security.

139
00:09:34,983 --> 00:09:37,439
The platform owns the reusable mechanism.

140
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:39,897
The domain still owns business logic

141
00:09:40,897 --> 00:09:43,574
Adopt incrementally and
review failure modes.

142
00:09:43,626 --> 00:09:45,092
This is very important.

143
00:09:45,461 --> 00:09:48,404
Event-driven architecture
should be adopted incrementally.

144
00:09:48,509 --> 00:09:52,176
Start with one bounded business
event, define the owner, contract,

145
00:09:52,188 --> 00:09:55,876
consumers, failure modes, idempotency,
replay, and observability.

146
00:09:56,351 --> 00:09:58,676
Then expand once those
patterns are proven.

147
00:09:59,134 --> 00:10:02,489
And not every interaction
needs asynchronous processing.

148
00:10:02,708 --> 00:10:06,253
If an immediate authoritative
response is required, a synchronous

149
00:10:06,258 --> 00:10:09,159
API may still be the better choice

150
00:10:10,159 --> 00:10:11,138
Key takeaways.

151
00:10:11,201 --> 00:10:12,737
The main points are simple.

152
00:10:13,137 --> 00:10:17,364
Event-driven architecture improves
decoupling and scalability, but

153
00:10:17,370 --> 00:10:21,980
introduces new challenges around delivery,
ordering, consistency, and recovery.

154
00:10:22,892 --> 00:10:28,043
design for duplicate processing, make
ownership and consistency explicit.

155
00:10:28,316 --> 00:10:34,326
Use patterns like, outbox, CQRS,
event sourcing, and sagas only

156
00:10:34,329 --> 00:10:36,251
where they solve a real problem.

157
00:10:36,921 --> 00:10:42,077
And use platform engineering to make
reliability, observability, recovery,

158
00:10:42,078 --> 00:10:45,175
and governance u- reusable across teams

159
00:10:46,175 --> 00:10:51,559
To close, resilient event-driven
systems are not created by any single

160
00:10:51,562 --> 00:10:53,490
technology or a messaging platform.

161
00:10:54,046 --> 00:10:59,883
They come from clear ownership, reliable
delivery, explicit consistency, idempotent

162
00:10:59,894 --> 00:11:04,662
processing, observability, recovery,
and disciplined contract evolution.

163
00:11:05,276 --> 00:11:08,398
Platform engineering makes
these capabilities consistent

164
00:11:08,406 --> 00:11:11,171
and reusable across teams.

165
00:11:11,549 --> 00:11:16,212
The goal is to make resilient
distributed dis-- system design easier

166
00:11:16,214 --> 00:11:18,923
to build, operate, and evolve at scale.

167
00:11:19,979 --> 00:11:21,555
Thank you so much for listening in.

168
00:11:21,832 --> 00:11:22,964
Appreciate all of you.

169
00:11:23,249 --> 00:11:23,828
Thanks.
